  "take-off",
  "landing",
  "stand"
 ],
 "rulebook": "The standing long jump (standing broad jump) of school and club fitness test batteries (Eurofit, ALPHA-Fitness, the Chinese National Student Physical Health Standard): the feet side by side behind the take-off line, a two-foot take-off with no step or preliminary hop, a landing on the feet; the jump is measured from the take-off line to the nearest point of contact at the landing",
 "deviations": [
  "the athlete cannot walk up to the line: any step before the take-off is a foul, so the robot is placed with the front ends of its feet 0.05 m behind the line (a rigid foot that pivots on its toes at the push-off swings up to 3 cm forward of where they stood)",
  "the measurement: the mark is the contact nearest the line of any part of the robot from the landing until it has stood for 1.0 s (a step or a hand put back shortens the jump; a fall voids it), where the test measures the heels at the landing",
  "a jump is told from a loss of contact by two numbers the test does not have: a flight of at least 0.1 s in which the pelvis rises at least 0.05 m above its height at lift-off",
  "the qualifying mark, 1.32 m, is the robot's own standing height (FAMILIES.md); the school standards scale with the athlete: adolescent pass marks are roughly 1.0 to 1.3 times body height"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Do a standing long jump: from a standstill behind the take-off line, take off from both feet without a step, land on your feet at least your own height beyond the line, and stand.",
  "rules": "The take-off line is x = 0; you jump towards +x. Your jump is your first flight (nothing of you touches the ground) of at least 0.1 s. Until then: no part of a foot that is on the ground may be on or over the line (the whole foot counts, also the parts of it that are in the air); no run-up and no step: every point where a foot touches the ground must lie within 0.03 m of the place where that foot's sole stood at t = 0, and neither foot may be off the ground for more than 0.05 s while the other is on it (so both feet leave the ground within 0.05 s of each other: a two-foot take-off). Rising on the toes is allowed. The flight must be a jump: your pelvis has to rise at least 0.05 m above its height at lift-off. The first contact after the flight must be with your feet only. The mark is the point nearest the take-off line at which any part of you touches the ground from that landing until you have stood; it must be at least 1.32 m beyond the line. After the landing you may step; you succeed once you stand for 1.0 s without a break: only your feet on the ground, your torso within about 20 degrees of vertical, your pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s. A fall fails the attempt at any time: your pelvis lower than 0.35 m, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground. You fail on a foot on or over the line, a step or shuffle before the take-off, a foot that leaves the ground before the other, a flight that is not a jump, a landing that is not on the feet, a mark short of 1.32 m, a fall, or after 20 s.",
  "venue": "You stand facing +x with your feet side by side, their front ends 0.05 m behind the take-off line (white); your pelvis is 0.164 m behind the line. The floor is level and firm; a yellow line is painted across it at the qualifying mark.",
  "difficulty": "Neither built-in gait can jump. The take-off needs both legs' full power in one coordinated push with the body leaning well ahead of the feet, and a jump of the robot's own height comes down at more than 3 m/s: the legs have to absorb it without a hand or the pelvis going down."
 },
